The Apache 2.0 licence at the repository root does not apply here. The root
NOTICE explains which packages are open; this file is the per-directory
boundary that licence line refers to, and it exists because a single root
licence cannot describe a repository that deliberately contains both.

What is open and what is not, and the hard rule that a feature never moves from
open to paid, are in docs/DECISIONS.md section 5. In short: the interception
spine is open, which is the part a security reviewer has to be able to read
before putting it in a write path. The hosted server, the entitlement gate, the
merchant of record, multi-workspace hosting and SSO are the paid side.

Why this directory is closed, in the terms section 5 uses to decide it. The
operational test asks whether a solo developer protecting one Postgres could do
their job without it. The answer for everything here is yes, which is what puts
it on the paid side: a solo developer needs the proxy, the policy engine, the
ledger and the verifier, all of which are open, and none of which is here.

Specifically, this directory holds the account broker and its sealed session
cookies, the owner-scoped encrypted store holding customer documents and provider
credentials, the OAuth round trips that put a provider token behind that store,
the recovery and document surfaces that run on top of it, and the paid
entitlement gate in capability-gate.mjs together with the merchant of record in
billing.mjs that funds it. None of it is the interception spine; all of it reads
the spine rather than replacing it, so a reader who cannot see this directory can
still read everything that decides whether an agent's write is allowed.

The independent verifier, which is what an auditor needs in order to check VOID
without trusting VOID, is not here. It is @void/verifier, it has no dependency
on this repository, and that independence is the point.
